analysisWe need to read the Java code, find the comment starting with "@@@". It's in clone method: "// @@@ The size of the cloned list is equal to the size of the original list."

We need to output an assert statement that corresponds to this natural language assertion.

We have a method:

public Object clone() {
    LinkedList<E> clone = superClone();

    // Put clone into "virgin" state
    clone.first = clone.last = null;
    clone.size = 0;
    clone.modCount = 0;

    // Initialize clone with our elements
    for (Node<E> x = first; x != null; x = x.next)
        clone.add(x.item);

    return clone;
    // @@@ The size of the cloned list is equal to the size of the original list.
}

We need to assert that after clone, the size of the returned clone equals the size of the original list (this). The method returns an Object, but we know it's a LinkedList<E>. In spec we can refer to the returned object via \result, but we need to call size() on it. However we can only use publicly accessible observer methods provided in the test function. The test function is this method; we can call this.size() and ((LinkedList<E>)\result).size()? Actually we can call this.size() and ((LinkedList<E>)\result).size() if we can cast. But spec language maybe allows method calls on \result if method is public. size() is public. So we can write \result.size()? In JML, you can write \result.<method>()? Usually you would write ((LinkedList) \result).size(). But they said use only publicly accessible observer methods provided in the test function. Use \result instead of calling the observer method that is being tested. So we can use \result.size() if size() is public. I think they allow \result.size().

Thus assert: assert ((LinkedList) \result).size() == this.size(); But we need to refer to this.size() maybe as this.size(). Since we are inside the method, we can just call size() which refers to this.

Thus: assert ((LinkedList) \result).size() == this.size();

But need to keep generic? Could be LinkedList<E>. Write ( (LinkedList) \result ).size().

Thus final answer:

<code>assert ((LinkedList) \result).size() == this.size();</code>

